Falsifies `nifefs::verification::a_short_image_is_refused_not_indexed`.

The defect: the length guard covers the first block rather than the whole directory span. The
header does live in block zero, so "the image must be at least a block" reads as the right
check; what it misses is that the entry table runs on to the end of `DIR_BLOCKS`, and the
comment above the line is the only thing that says so.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert_eq!(Fs::parse(&image).err(), Some(Error::Truncated))` on
an image one byte short of the directory span, alongside an unwinding assertion in `parse`'s entry
loop. The parse accepts the short image and goes on to index its directory, which is a filesystem
server trusting an image a client handed it. Red in 19.6 s of verification on 2026-09-24.

This record needs the harness's `#[kani::unwind(9)]`. Without it the replay never reaches a
verdict (#1156's CI run was cancelled at 45 minutes), which is why #1217 briefly recorded a
reordering in its place; the harness's comment has the measurements.

diff --git a/crates/nifefs/src/lib.rs b/crates/nifefs/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/nifefs/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/nifefs/src/lib.rs
@@ -262,7 +262,7 @@ impl<'a> Fs<'a> {
         // The directory blocks must be present before any entry offset (up to HEADER_LEN +
         // MAX_FILES*ENTRY_LEN, which sits inside DIR_BLOCKS) is read; this guard is what makes
         // those reads sound.
-        if image.len() < DIR_BLOCKS * BLOCK {
+        if image.len() < BLOCK {
             return Err(Error::Truncated);
         }
